sar test code ever runs:\n\n```\nUncaught exception while running isolate initialization hooks:\njava.lang.StackOverflowError\n```\n\nThe cycle is:\n`RuntimeDynamicAccessMetadata.canonicalConditions` → `Arrays.copyOf` on a `Class[]`-typed conditions array → `Array.newInstance(Class.class, …)` → the tracer's `shouldTraceReflectionArrayType(Class.class)` → `Class.class.getDynamicAccessMetadata()` → which re-enters the *same* decode path for `java.lang.Class` → `canonicalConditions` again → forever.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n`runNativeTraceImage` builds the image with two **experimental** flags together: `-H:+MetadataTracingSupport` (traces every reflective array creation) and `--exact-reachability-metadata` (every type's dynamic-access metadata is condition-gated and lazily decoded). The tracer's array-creation hook needs to know whether `Class[]` creation should be traced, which means decoding `java.lang.Class`'s own metadata — but that decode itself needs to create a `Class[]` array to canonicalize its conditions, re-entering the same not-yet-cached decode. It's a non-reentrant decode racing against itself.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\nI ruled out a Pulsar-specific cause with two experiments (then reverted both — the agent-collected JSON is back to its original 239 entries):\n1. **Removed the two `java.lang.Class` reflect entries** (from Jackson/Gson record-detection, used by `JDK14UtilInnerRecordAccessorTest`/`ReflectionHelperInnerRecordSupportedHelperTest`) and force-rebuilt the trace image — identical crash, same line numbers.\n2. **Pointed `metadataConfigDirs` at an empty `{}`** and force-rebuilt — isolate init now succeeds, but the *same* `StackOverflowError` resurfaces moments later while JUnit Platform Native tries to report a (now-expected) test-discovery failure, because printing that exception's stack trace creates a `StackTraceElement[]` and hits the identical recursive path.\n\nSo this isn't a missing/inactive metadata condition, and it isn't native-image-unsupported test behavior (dynamic proxies, classloaders, etc.) — it's a recursion bug inside GraalVM 25.4.4's own experimental tracer/exact-metadata combination that any sufficiently populated reachability metadata (even JUnit's own bundled config) will trigger. Neither of the two remedies I was given — fix a metadata condition, or rewrite/remove a generated test — can address a VM-internal non-reentrant decode.\n\nSince I'm pinned to this exact GraalVM build and told not to switch, I haven't tried a different version to confirm whether it's fixed upstream. How would you like to proceed — report this as a GraalVM bug and park this PR, or do you want me to check whether a different installed GraalVM candidate (e.g. `26.ea.13-graal`) avoids this, as a diagnostic (not a replacement for the pinned build)?","ttft_ms":5231,"type":"result","duration_ms":1302221,"uuid":"f0091b9d-f16e-41bd-b16c-db230c2d41b0","ttft_stream_ms":1888,"time_to_request_ms":142,"first_content_frame_ms":1888,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}

